d alaskan americans  um there are two areas in which they  find themselves disadvantaged  the first though one like does not like  to generalize  but as a general demographic group  minorities fi find themselves because of  their economic  and employment situations in a situation  where it's less easy for them to protect  themselves from getting infected  they have less of a chance of sitting in  front of a computer  teleworking as opposed to being outside  with jobs that require exposure  so their chances of getting infected  are greater than the general population  that's disadvantage number one  disadvantage number two  is when they do get infected  given the social determinants of  medicine that have been around for so  long  they have a much greater incidence and  prevalence  of comorbidities which lead to a poorer  outcome  diabetes hypertension  obesity cardiovascular disease  those kinds of things which put them in  a higher risk  category so it's what we call a double  negative whammy that they get what can  we do now  and what can we do in the long run what  we can do now  is concentrate resources in the  areas where they are demographically  over-represented so that they could have  easy access to testing  better access to health care so that if  and when they do get infected  they can get into proper care early  enough  to maybe mitigate some of the negative  consequences that we can do now  the longer range is do we have a  commitment which i hope we do  to eliminate health disparities which is  a  decade-long commitment you're not going  to do that  overnight there are so many things that  you can do  to modify the reasons why  they have more hypertension they have  more diabetes  they have more obesity so we can do  stuff now  and we can make a commitment to do  things in the long run  thanks claire okay next up we've got  dominic gordon dominic introduce  yourself  hello my name is dominic gordon and i'm  a a freshman in the school of foreign  servic